{"context":"MCP currently exposes 22 tools combining retrieval, authoring, lifecycle mutation, snapshot mutation, assignment, feedback, and external GitHub publishing. Tool schemas, required fields, descriptions, dispatch, and tests are partly maintained separately. Review found weak schemas, mixed trust boundaries, broad write access, publish mode multiplexing, deprecated view exposure, absolute path leakage, and draft_update bypassing the Authoring API. Skills and commands are already embedded and partially exposed through MCP resources, but docs and installed copies are stale or incomplete.","decision":"Refine MCP as a thin, agent-facing adapter over transport-neutral EKA application capabilities. Prioritize a small read core: status, context, get, domain, code_context, validate, integrity_check. Classify tools as read, local-write, canonical-write, or external-side-effect. Keep read access available by default; gate writes by explicit policy and exact approval; disable external publishing by default. Make tool descriptors authoritative for name, schema, risk class, deprecation, and dispatch. Use strict schemas with enums, oneOf, bounds, and deliberate unknown-field policy. Split single-draft publish from batch publish in the new contract. Remove deprecated view from advertised tools while retaining compatibility dispatch temporarily. Return logical/relative identifiers instead of absolute host paths. Route draft_update through a runtime-owned Authoring API with optimistic concurrency, or remove it until that API exists. Keep MCP and CLI over shared EKA application APIs; MCP is not the domain model or source of truth. Use MCP resources as primary distribution path for EKA skill and command guidance without requiring per-agent installation. Resources are discoverable content, not executable skills: provide compact manifest/index and bootstrap resource, lazy loading, versioning, annotations, and explicit fallback to inline guidance or CLI installation for clients that cannot surface resources. Commands execute through MCP tools; semantics remain in shared APIs. Update supported protocol behavior based on current MCP client/spec compatibility rather than hardcoding stale assumptions.","consequences":"Smaller tool catalog lowers agent selection and tool-definition token cost. Explicit risk classes make authorization and confirmation enforceable. Shared descriptors reduce schema/dispatch drift. Strict schemas improve refusal clarity but require compatibility policy for unknown fields and legacy clients. Separating external effects reduces blast radius. Resource delivery removes duplicate manual skill copies for resource-aware clients and reduces version drift, but cannot guarantee automatic loading because MCP clients control resource selection and model-context insertion. Resource metadata and bootstrap content must stay compact. Some existing MCP clients may need migration from view, publish combinations, paths, permissive fields, and installed skill assumptions.","alternativesConsidered":"Keep all CLI commands exposed as MCP tools rejected due to tool sprawl and mixed trust boundaries. MCP subprocess proxy rejected as long-term architecture because of latency, error coupling, and text parsing. Agent-controlled confirmed boolean rejected as sufficient human approval. RAG/code search as MCP core rejected; code intelligence stays bounded and provider-neutral. Manual installation for every agent rejected as default because it creates copies and version drift; retain as compatibility fallback for clients without usable MCP resource support. Representing skills as executable tools rejected; guidance belongs in resources/prompts, operations belong in tools."}